Encounter — Day 1: Professional Definition
Definition

An encounter is an interaction between a patient and a healthcare organization, provider, or healthcare service during which healthcare services are provided or managed. In clinical information systems, an encounter commonly represents a specific episode of contact, such as an outpatient visit, emergency department visit, or inpatient stay. An encounter can contain or link to clinical information such as diagnoses, procedures, observations, medications, orders, and providers. The exact definition and structure of an encounter can vary across EHR systems, data models, and interoperability standards.

Category

Clinical data / EHR / Healthcare analytics

Why it matters

Encounter data is often a foundational part of healthcare analytics.

Analysts may use encounters to:

Count outpatient, inpatient, and emergency visits
Analyze healthcare utilization
Connect clinical events to a specific patient visit
Calculate metrics such as ED utilization or visit volume
Link diagnoses, procedures, providers, and services
Build patient cohorts
Analyze care patterns over time

For example, an analyst might ask:

"How many emergency department encounters occurred each month?"

or:

"How many patients had at least three outpatient encounters during the year?"

Review note

The meaning and structure of an encounter can differ by EHR, organization, data model, or interoperability standard. Do not assume that every system represents a "visit" as exactly one encounter.

Review note: Verify this detail against the relevant authoritative source when working with a specific EHR, data model, or interoperability implementation.
